; The fetch stack (P6): URL fetching behind the Net authority grant, the
; extraction-backend contract, and the JSON/table renderers a page can take.
; It is its own library — [seekwright.fetch], wrapped as [Seekwright_fetch] —
; the way [lib/providers/] is [seekwright.providers], because the dependency
; direction is fixed the same way: nothing in [lib/] may name a fetch backend,
; so whatever builds one (the engine, P5, or the factory) depends on this
; library, never the reverse.
;
; All HTTP goes through a [Mcp_caps.Authority.Net] grant — no raw cohttp — and
; the fetcher receives the grant plus the extraction backend injected, with no
; default backend, so importing this library never imports a backend's
; dependencies. There is no page cache in v1 (deferred by plan review).
;
; The stdlib extraction backend sits in [extractors/] with its own stanza
; ([seekwright.fetch.stdlib]) for the same reason [lib/providers/] has one:
; a backend is a separate install unit — Python ships trafilatura as an
; extra — and the directory keeps the layout the Python tree has.
(library
 (name seekwright_fetch)
 (public_name seekwright.fetch)
 ; [seekwright.text] is the port's one codepoint walk and [repr] pass (style
 ; review H1 and H2). It used to be a copy here and a copy in [seekwright],
 ; because neither big library may link the other; the walk therefore sits on a
 ; stdlib-only floor under both.
 (libraries
  seekwright.text
  mcp-caps
  uri
  yojson))
